WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:08.240
Hey David, how will the 15% primary threshold
effect who wins the democratic primary?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.470
So there's another way.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:15.690
I think that what people are trying to get
to is, is the 15% threshold going to screw

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.820
this candidate or that candidate?

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.470
So there's a couple of things to understand.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:26.330
In the democratic primaries, there is a 15%
threshold where if you don't get 15% of the

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.949
vote, you don't get any of the delegates.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:34.941
This is particularly important in the bigger
States because in the smaller States, there

00:00:34.941 --> 00:00:40.370
are often so few delegates to go around that
besides the, the sort of momentum impact of

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:46.539
it, it's not really mathematically that important
to be getting delegates from the small States

00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:52.390
as long as you can continue justifying staying
in the race if, but if no candidate meets

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:59.480
the 15% threshold, what the democratic party
rules is that the minimum to receive delegates

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.100
becomes half of the front runners vote share.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.970
So imagine that you have a state where a candidate
wins with 14% of the votes.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.930
That's the plurality 14% and then you know,
someone else has 13 and 12 whatever it adds

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:22.040
up to a hundred if the winner has 14% everybody
would 7% or more delegates gets delegates.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.660
So that's in the rules.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.590
But let's put that aside for a second, cause
that's not the most likely scenario.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:34.790
In later States, in really big States, you
could see this become a factor, uh, in the

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.470
broader delegate math.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:42.860
So imagine that California goes the way one
recent national poll says, imagine that Biden

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.950
gets 23% of the vote in California.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.030
Warren gets 18 and Bernie gets 14 hypothetically,
right?

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.460
Because Bernie didn't get 15% of the vote.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:02:01.630
Bernie gets zero, which means that Biden gets
his 23% uh, Warren gets her 18% it actually

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.460
can go up once you eliminate people sub 15
but ignore that for now.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.670
But the really important thing about this
is that Bernie and Warren in this scenario

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:16.870
have 32% a strong progressive contingent that
has more support than Biden, but that 15%

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:22.740
threshold could make it so that the progressive
candidates get kneecap and end up getting

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:30.410
zero in some States because one of them didn't
get to the 15% this is why I get emails from

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:36.250
people saying, David, at some point shouldn't
burn your Warren drop out because if they

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:42.360
make it so that neither or only one of them
gets 15% and the other ends up getting no

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.010
delegates as a result of that, isn't it going
to help the establishment?

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:54.350
Basically Joe Biden when the answer is yes,
but this is why it's good for the early primaries

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:58.470
to help one candidate build momentum over
the other.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:03.569
My most, uh, the most likely scenario for
how this is going to go is that after New

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:09.120
Hampshire, uh, after, sorry, Iowa, New Hampshire,
Nevada and South Carolina, after they all

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.220
vote, I think there is going to be a clear,
a clear momentum advantage for either Bernie

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.770
or Warren as the progressive candidates.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:22.440
And I think that in the end, the one with
the momentum is going to take a significant

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.770
lead over the one that doesn't have it.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.650
And this issue of splitting the progressive
vote will be less and less of an issue as

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.470
the campaign goes forward.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:35.080
I could be wrong and if that doesn't happen,
then it is possible that the progressive wing

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.380
of the democratic party will be hurt by this.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.910
But my expectation is that the first four
primaries will establish which of the two

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:46.370
progressives is going to go the distance and
that then you will very quickly start to see

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:48.770
support coalescing around one or the other.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.040
If you think it won't go that way, then I
want to hear from you.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.680
Either leave a comment, if you're watching
on YouTube

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:00.360
or send me a tweet at D Pacman, we've got
